{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show an upswing (higher highs / positive slope), add an upward level shift to the whole path (≈ +1.5 to +3.0 €/t), strongest in days 5–20 where underprediction is largest.",
    "Trend/volatility underreaction: the model smooths moves and misses rally amplitude; increase forecast sensitivity to recent momentum (e.g., scale recent 10-day return contribution up) so peaks are higher and arrive sooner.",
    "Lag around spikes: after a sharp up-move in history, the model’s peak occurs later and lower than realized; pull forward the peak timing by a few days and lift the near-term (days 1–10) by ~+0.5 to +2.0 €/t when momentum is strong.",
    "Late-horizon mean-reversion error: the model often stays too high at the far end (days ~25–30) when the realized path rolls over; apply stronger downward mean reversion in the tail (reduce days 25–30 by ~1–3 €/t) when recent volatility is elevated or when the model projects a continued rise into the tail.",
    "Asymmetry: corrections should be regime-dependent—apply larger positive corrections during sustained rallies, but allow faster negative adjustment in the last third of the horizon after extended run-ups (to avoid overestimating persistence)."
  ]
}
